[1] In January 1925, Curtis Welch, the only doctor in Nome, Alaska, recognized the signs of diphtheria, a contagious disease that was especially deadly to children. [2] The town's small supply of antitoxin{{1|, the serum that could stop the outbreak,}} had expired. [3] Nome was icebound for the winter, and flying a plane through the extreme cold was considered too risky.

[4] {{2|Nenana is a town in the interior of Alaska.}} [5] Fresh serum was carried by train from Anchorage to Nenana, where the rail line ended, still about 674 miles from Nome. [6] From there, some twenty mushers and about 150 dogs carried it in a relay, each team running its stretch and {{3|then afterward passing}} the package to the next.

[7] The relay ran through blizzards and temperatures far below zero. [8] The longest and most dangerous stretch belonged to Leonhard Seppala and his lead dog, Togo, who crossed the frozen, shifting ice of Norton Sound. {{4|}}

[9] Early on February 2, 1925, Gunnar Kaasen and his lead dog, Balto, {{5|arrive}} in Nome with the serum, five and a half days after it had left Nenana. [10] {{6|Like many sled dogs of the time,}} Balto became famous almost overnight: newspapers across the country told the story, and later that year a bronze statue of him was unveiled in New York City's Central Park. [11] {{7|Many mushers have argued since then that Togo, who covered far more ground, deserved more of the credit.}}

[12] Today, the run is remembered in the Iditarod, a sled dog race of roughly a thousand miles from Anchorage to Nome that honors the serum relay in part. [13] {{8|In 1925, though, no one was racing for a trophy; the mushers were racing to save lives.}}

[1] In late 1782, Joseph-Michel Montgolfier, the son of a paper manufacturer in southern France, noticed that a small fabric bag held over a fire {{1|rose toward the ceiling, he}} began to wonder whether a much larger bag could lift a person. [2] Working with his brother Jacques-Étienne, he built a series of larger balloons from paper and cloth.

[3] On June 4, 1783, the brothers {{2|showed people what they had made}} in their hometown of Annonay. [4] Word of the event reached Paris, and King Louis XVI asked to see a flight.

[5] On September 19, 1783, at the palace of Versailles, a balloon carried its first {{3|passengers, a sheep, a duck, and a rooster.}} [6] The animals landed safely about eight minutes later, which suggested that the air high above the ground was not {{4|totally deadly or anything}}. {{5|}}

[7] Two months later, on November 21, 1783, the science teacher Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d'Arlandes became the first people to fly in a free balloon. [8] {{6|Rising from a garden on the edge of Paris, the city spread out below them.}} [9] They drifted {{7|for a duration of about twenty-five minutes in length}} and landed some five miles away.

[10] {{8|Hydrogen is the lightest of all the chemical elements.}} [11] Only ten days later, the physicist Jacques Charles flew a balloon filled with hydrogen for about two hours. [12] {{9|Today, hot-air balloons are flown mainly for sport and sightseeing.}}
